WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.420 --> 00:00:01.839
Who is Aquaman?

00:00:01.839 --> 00:00:06.140
If you've seen Justice League, you know him
as the ludicrously muscular Jason Momoa.

00:00:06.140 --> 00:00:09.790
If you grew up watching Superfriends, you
remember him as the blond guy in an orange

00:00:09.790 --> 00:00:12.230
shirt who didn't usually have much to do.

00:00:12.230 --> 00:00:16.219
If you've encountered internet humor in the
past 20 years, you've probably heard that

00:00:16.219 --> 00:00:17.550
he talks to fish.

00:00:17.550 --> 00:00:18.550
That's not all, though.

00:00:18.550 --> 00:00:23.449
He's been a king, a husband, a father, an
amputee, and an exile in his decades-long

00:00:23.449 --> 00:00:24.449
existence.

00:00:24.449 --> 00:00:26.500
So, he's a pretty busy fish-guy.

00:00:26.500 --> 00:00:28.490
Here is the untold truth of Aquaman.

00:00:28.490 --> 00:00:29.490
"I'll try to make this as quick and painless
as possible"

00:00:29.490 --> 00:00:30.900
Alternate origins

00:00:30.900 --> 00:00:35.470
Everybody knows that Aquaman's from Atlantis,
but in his first appearance in 1941's More

00:00:35.470 --> 00:00:38.899
Fun Comics #73, that wasn't the case at all.

00:00:38.899 --> 00:00:43.149
His father was a world-famous undersea explorer
and researcher.

00:00:43.149 --> 00:00:46.489
Aquaman's mother died when he was a baby,
and his father took him to live under the

00:00:46.489 --> 00:00:47.489
sea.

00:00:47.489 --> 00:00:51.760
Using the ancient science of that forgotten
civilization, he raised Aquaman to live comfortably

00:00:51.760 --> 00:00:55.909
under the sea, giving him the ability to breathe
water and the name "Aquaman."

00:00:55.909 --> 00:00:59.519
It was only later stories that made Aquaman
an Atlantean prince.

00:00:59.519 --> 00:01:03.789
In the 1940s he was simply too busy fighting
Nazi U-boat captains to have that complex

00:01:03.789 --> 00:01:05.409
a backstory.

00:01:05.409 --> 00:01:06.890
Alternate alternate origins

00:01:06.890 --> 00:01:11.780
Adventure Comics #260 established that Aquaman
was the son of a lighthouse keeper named Tom

00:01:11.780 --> 00:01:14.670
Curry and a mysterious woman who came from
the sea.

00:01:14.670 --> 00:01:19.140
On her deathbed, she revealed herself as an
exile from Atlantis, and that their son Arthur

00:01:19.140 --> 00:01:21.600
had inherited her aquatic powers.

00:01:21.600 --> 00:01:25.579
Later stories would establish the mysterious
woman as the Princess of Atlantis, making

00:01:25.579 --> 00:01:27.740
Arthur Curry the heir to its throne.

00:01:27.740 --> 00:01:33.130
In 1989's Aquaman Special #1, this origin
was revised yet again so that Aquaman was

00:01:33.130 --> 00:01:37.520
born in Atlantis, left to die in the open
ocean as an infant, and later adopted by the

00:01:37.520 --> 00:01:38.659
lighthouse keeper.

00:01:38.659 --> 00:01:42.350
Arthur being raised by a lighthouse keeper
has remained one of the more consistent details

00:01:42.350 --> 00:01:46.509
of Aquaman's origin stories going back to
the beginning, even when later retcons would

00:01:46.509 --> 00:01:51.990
add in a biological father who was an undersea
wizard and other familial weirdness.

00:01:51.990 --> 00:01:53.859
Aquaman and his octo-pal

00:01:53.859 --> 00:01:58.150
Most people remember Aquaman's sidekick as
Aqualad, a dark-haired teen in red and blue.

00:01:58.150 --> 00:01:59.150
"Aquaman!

00:01:59.150 --> 00:02:05.200
With his teenage ally Aqualad, guards and
defends all that lives in the sea against

00:02:05.200 --> 00:02:06.960
the forces of evil."

00:02:06.960 --> 00:02:11.980
Aqualad first appeared in 1960, but Aquaman
met his first lasting ally years earlier,

00:02:11.980 --> 00:02:13.340
in 1956.

00:02:13.340 --> 00:02:18.410
In Adventure Comics #229, Aquaman is inspired
by a Canadian Mountie's relationship with

00:02:18.410 --> 00:02:23.150
his horse, so he holds a contest to see which
sea creature should become his sidekick.

00:02:23.150 --> 00:02:25.690
The winner ends up being an octopus named
Topo.

00:02:25.690 --> 00:02:29.680
Since Aquaman's solo adventures pretty much
all take place at sea, an octopus has more

00:02:29.680 --> 00:02:31.640
to contribute than you might expect.

00:02:31.640 --> 00:02:36.970
Even after Aqualad came on the scene in Adventure
Comics #269, that clever octopus has stuck

00:02:36.970 --> 00:02:39.890
around as a member of the supporting cast.

00:02:39.890 --> 00:02:41.260
Aquaman uncovered

00:02:41.260 --> 00:02:45.400
You've almost certainly seen the cover to
The Brave and the Bold #28, the first appearance

00:02:45.400 --> 00:02:47.180
of the Justice League of America.

00:02:47.180 --> 00:02:50.680
Now here's what you might not know: that first
Justice League cover where they're fighting

00:02:50.680 --> 00:02:54.959
Starro is the first time Aquaman ever appeared
on a comic book cover.

00:02:54.959 --> 00:02:59.440
Sure, Aquaman had been around for nineteen
years, but he'd never had his own comic.

00:02:59.440 --> 00:03:03.299
Superboy had been the cover feature on Adventure
Comics for as long as he and Aquaman had been

00:03:03.299 --> 00:03:04.530
featured in the book.

00:03:04.530 --> 00:03:09.230
Before that, Aquaman was overshadowed by Green
Arrow in the pages of More Fun Comics.

00:03:09.230 --> 00:03:13.599
Being a minor hero whose adventures were mostly
at sea, Aquaman had never joined the Justice

00:03:13.599 --> 00:03:16.520
Society of America, DC's first superteam.

00:03:16.520 --> 00:03:20.439
By the time that team was revamped with a
new name, however, Aquaman had survived for

00:03:20.439 --> 00:03:24.530
so long that his veteran status made him a
shoe-in for the new team.

00:03:24.530 --> 00:03:28.379
Making him an active part of the DC Universe
increased his popularity to the degree that

00:03:28.380 --> 00:03:32.380
he finally got his own solo comic starting
in 1962.

00:03:32.380 --> 00:03:33.620
Aqua-married

00:03:34.010 --> 00:03:39.260
Having his own title in the 1960's gave Aquaman
more room to build a complete supporting cast,

00:03:39.260 --> 00:03:42.920
including an ongoing love interest, something
he'd never had before.

00:03:42.920 --> 00:03:47.790
Mera was introduced in Aquaman #11 as a beautiful
queen from another dimension with the ability

00:03:47.790 --> 00:03:49.260
to control water.

00:03:49.260 --> 00:03:53.879
In issue 18, Aquaman finally takes the throne
of Atlantis, but learns that he must immediately

00:03:53.879 --> 00:03:57.860
choose a queen, so he and Mera get married
in that same issue.

00:03:57.860 --> 00:04:01.530
Superhero weddings were largely unheard-of
when this happened in 1964.

00:04:01.530 --> 00:04:06.360
The most famous superhero wedding of the 1960s
was Mr. Fantastic and the Invisible Girl tying

00:04:06.360 --> 00:04:13.109
the knot in Fantastic Four, but that didn't
happen until 1965, a year after the big Aqua-wedding.

00:04:13.109 --> 00:04:16.130
Aquaman established the tropes that would
turn up again in Fantastic Four and other

00:04:16.130 --> 00:04:21.870
wedding stories, like costumed guest stars
as guests and of course supervillain attacks.

00:04:21.870 --> 00:04:22.870
Baby on board

00:04:22.870 --> 00:04:27.050
Once Aquaman and Queen Mera were married,
it was only a matter of time before they produced

00:04:27.050 --> 00:04:28.050
an heir.

00:04:28.050 --> 00:04:32.670
Arthur Curry Jr., usually called Aquababy,
was born in Aquaman #23.

00:04:32.670 --> 00:04:37.530
In the 1960s Aquaman was breaking new ground
just by settling down and starting a family.

00:04:37.530 --> 00:04:41.530
Of course he was also one of the only superheroes
who ruled his own kingdom.

00:04:41.530 --> 00:04:45.420
Aquaman had always stood apart from other
heroes due to the aquatic nature of his adventures,

00:04:45.420 --> 00:04:48.770
so it made sense for his stories to evolve
along their own path instead of following

00:04:48.770 --> 00:04:50.830
the usual superhero tropes.

00:04:50.830 --> 00:04:55.200
But by the 1970s, Aquaman's idyllic undersea
life would be coming to an end.

00:04:55.200 --> 00:05:00.451
In Adventure Comics #452, Aquaman has a showdown
with his greatest villain, Black Manta, who

00:05:00.451 --> 00:05:03.930
has kidnapped Aquababy and put him in a slow-moving
death trap.

00:05:03.930 --> 00:05:08.080
That baby's a goner unless Aquaman and Aqualad
fight to the death.

00:05:08.080 --> 00:05:10.680
"Black Manta!

00:05:10.680 --> 00:05:12.440
That's not very neighborly!"

00:05:12.440 --> 00:05:16.280
It's fairly standard supervillain fare and
it seems like Aquaman will get everyone out

00:05:16.280 --> 00:05:17.780
alive like usual.

00:05:17.780 --> 00:05:20.390
Unfortunately, it doesn't go that way this
time.

00:05:20.390 --> 00:05:23.070
Aquaman disrupts Black Manta's plans, but
it's too late.

00:05:23.070 --> 00:05:26.940
Arthur Curry Jr. is already gone when they
get him out of the suffocating bubble.

00:05:26.940 --> 00:05:31.140
Ever since Aquaman's son died, his adventures
have taken on a darker tone, and his character

00:05:31.140 --> 00:05:32.560
has had a more brooding attitude.

00:05:32.560 --> 00:05:35.100
Give him a hand

00:05:35.100 --> 00:05:37.310
Aquaman would be reimagined again in the 1990s.

00:05:37.310 --> 00:05:42.050
He had already grown long hair and a beard,
and was soon one hand lighter after a villain

00:05:42.050 --> 00:05:46.160
shoved him into a pool of carnivorous fish
that were conveniently immune to Aquaman's

00:05:46.160 --> 00:05:47.160
telepathy.

00:05:47.160 --> 00:05:51.330
He replaced his hand with the hooked end of
a harpoon and soon abandoned his famous orange

00:05:51.330 --> 00:05:56.270
shirt, spending the next few years in a gray
armored half shirt and gray and green pants.

00:05:56.270 --> 00:06:00.801
By 2003, the aesthetic of '90s Arthur Curry
already felt tied to that decade, and it was

00:06:00.801 --> 00:06:01.930
time to move on.

00:06:01.930 --> 00:06:05.930
A new creative team finally gave the hero
a shave and haircut, as well as a new left

00:06:05.930 --> 00:06:06.930
hand.

00:06:06.930 --> 00:06:11.190
This hand was made of water, created by the
magic of the Lady of the Lake a magical being

00:06:11.190 --> 00:06:13.810
better known for helping out a different King
Arthur.

00:06:13.810 --> 00:06:15.770
Back to basics

00:06:15.770 --> 00:06:20.040
One of Aquaman's better known incarnations
was as a regular guest star on Batman: The

00:06:20.040 --> 00:06:21.040
Brave and the Bold.

00:06:21.040 --> 00:06:25.530
Voiced by John DiMaggio, this version of Arthur
Curry is bombastic, loud, and brash.

00:06:25.530 --> 00:06:30.290
He's supremely confident in his abilities
as a hero and adventurer, and not shy about

00:06:30.290 --> 00:06:31.800
sharing his confidence.

00:06:31.800 --> 00:06:35.450
Unlike other versions of Aquaman, who were
often moody and bitter, Brave and the Bold's

00:06:35.450 --> 00:06:37.480
Aquaman is endlessly upbeat and fun.

00:06:44.160 --> 00:06:49.020
Interestingly, the Aquaman of the 2017 Justice
League film, played by Jason Momoa, has a

00:06:49.020 --> 00:06:51.190
bit of Brave and the Bold Aquaman to him.

00:06:51.190 --> 00:06:55.270
He's a more serious character, but he also
has the brash confidence and daredevil attitude

00:06:55.270 --> 00:06:58.120
that made his animated counterpart such a
success.

00:06:58.120 --> 00:07:02.680
Mixing the masculine charisma of Jason Momoa
with the classic outsider perspective Aquaman

00:07:02.680 --> 00:07:07.130
has always had turned out to be a smash success,
and the character was a standout in Justice

00:07:07.130 --> 00:07:08.130
League.

00:07:08.130 --> 00:07:11.500
We'll have to see if the upcoming solo Aquaman
movie will manage to continue the character's

00:07:11.500 --> 00:07:12.530
watery winning streak.

